1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,687
It is February, twenty fifteen. In Dublin, an astrophysicist opens an email.

2
00:00:04,687 --> 00:00:10,129
It tells him he is one of a hundred people shortlisted to leave Earth forever.

3
00:00:11,266 --> 00:00:16,752
The destination is Mars. The trip is one way. There is no plan to ever bring him home.

4
00:00:17,899 --> 00:00:21,615
More than two hundred thousand people, he is told, wanted this seat.

5
00:00:21,615 --> 00:00:26,258
He is one of the chosen few. It is the proudest moment of his life.

6
00:00:27,399 --> 00:00:31,671
Within weeks, that same man will do something no other finalist dares to do.

7
00:00:31,671 --> 00:00:35,027
He will start pulling the whole thing apart from the inside.

8
00:00:36,166 --> 00:00:41,323
Because almost nothing he had been told was true. Not the science. Not the money.

9
00:00:41,323 --> 00:00:44,760
And, it turns out, not even the two hundred thousand.

10
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:47,928
This is the story of Mars One.

11
00:00:47,928 --> 00:00:55,461
The mission that sold the whole world a one-way ticket to another planet, and then quietly went bankrupt, for less than the price of a house.

12
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:58,167
You're watching mindwired.

13
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:00,057
Mars One.

14
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:03,708
Chapter one. The man who sold Mars.

15
00:01:04,833 --> 00:01:10,024
To understand how so many smart people believed this, you have to start with the man selling it.

16
00:01:10,024 --> 00:01:11,755
And he was not a crank.

17
00:01:12,899 --> 00:01:14,558
His name is Bas Lansdorp.

18
00:01:14,558 --> 00:01:22,852
A Dutch engineer, trained at Twente and Delft, who spent years chasing a wild idea in wind power, generating electricity from kites on a tether.

19
00:01:23,999 --> 00:01:27,370
It worked well enough to build a real company, Ampyx Power.

20
00:01:27,370 --> 00:01:31,966
And in twenty eleven, Lansdorp sold his stake in it to fund something far bigger.

21
00:01:33,099 --> 00:01:34,395
He was not alone.

22
00:01:34,395 --> 00:01:40,225
His co-founder was Arno Wielders, a physicist who had actually worked on spacecraft for the European Space Agency.

23
00:01:40,225 --> 00:01:42,816
This was not two kids with a website.

24
00:01:43,966 --> 00:01:49,382
And the vision was intoxicating. Not a flag and a footprint. Not a short visit.

25
00:01:49,382 --> 00:01:53,716
A permanent, growing human settlement on another world, in our own lifetime.

26
00:01:53,716 --> 00:01:56,243
Science fiction, finally with a launch date.

27
00:01:57,366 --> 00:02:02,008
In twenty twelve, they announced it to the world. A permanent human colony on Mars.

28
00:02:02,008 --> 00:02:05,412
The first crew of four would land in twenty twenty three.

29
00:02:06,533 --> 00:02:10,944
And the plan was staged and specific. A robotic scout first.

30
00:02:10,944 --> 00:02:16,157
Then supplies and habitats, waiting on the surface. And only then, four humans.

31
00:02:16,157 --> 00:02:21,371
On paper, it read like a real space program, with a real schedule.

32
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:25,509
And here was the idea that made it sound possible.

33
00:02:25,509 --> 00:02:29,421
The reason no one had colonized Mars, Lansdorp argued, was the trip home.

34
00:02:30,566 --> 00:02:36,763
Building a rocket that can lift off from the surface of Mars is brutally hard and brutally expensive.

35
00:02:36,763 --> 00:02:40,205
So Lansdorp's answer was simple, and chilling. Don't come back.

36
00:02:41,333 --> 00:02:45,548
Cut the return trip, and suddenly the mission fits inside technology that already exists.

37
00:02:45,548 --> 00:02:47,054
Or so the pitch went.

38
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:52,267
Mars One, Lansdorp said, would not even build the hardware.

39
00:02:52,267 --> 00:02:59,588
It would be an integrator, buying proven parts from the giants, from SpaceX, from Lockheed Martin, from Paragon.

40
00:03:00,733 --> 00:03:05,803
And the timing felt perfect. This was the dawn of the private space age.

41
00:03:05,803 --> 00:03:12,323
If a company like SpaceX could land its rockets upright, why couldn't a bold enough startup land people?

42
00:03:12,323 --> 00:03:14,134
The impossible suddenly felt overdue.

43
00:03:15,266 --> 00:03:18,481
It even paid real aerospace firms for real concept studies.

44
00:03:18,481 --> 00:03:22,659
On paper, it looked less like a fantasy and more like a startup.

45
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:28,847
Mars One was suddenly everywhere. CNN, the BBC, TED stages, magazine covers.

46
00:03:28,847 --> 00:03:35,998
For a while, it genuinely felt like watching the future being born, live, in front of us.

47
00:03:37,133 --> 00:03:39,641
Chapter two. A ticket to the stars.

48
00:03:40,766 --> 00:03:42,891
But a Mars colony costs billions.

49
00:03:42,891 --> 00:03:46,432
Lansdorp estimated six billion dollars just for the first crew.

50
00:03:46,432 --> 00:03:50,327
So where does a small Dutch foundation find six billion dollars?

51
00:03:51,466 --> 00:03:54,572
Lansdorp had two answers. The first was ordinary. Investors.

52
00:03:54,572 --> 00:03:59,747
The second was radical, and it was the one that made headlines around the world.

53
00:04:00,866 --> 00:04:06,364
The answer was the most audacious part of the whole plan. They would televise it.

54
00:04:06,364 --> 00:04:12,595
The selection, the training, the landing, a global reality show about the first humans on another world.

55
00:04:13,733 --> 00:04:17,204
Think of the Olympics, Lansdorp said. Billions of people watch.

56
00:04:17,204 --> 00:04:20,329
The broadcast rights alone could pay for the mission.

57
00:04:20,329 --> 00:04:24,495
It was the single most-watched event in human history, waiting to happen.

58
00:04:25,633 --> 00:04:30,152
Lansdorp put numbers on it that were almost hard to say out loud.

59
00:04:30,152 --> 00:04:35,714
Broadcasting the mission, he claimed, could eventually bring in as much as forty five billion dollars.

60
00:04:35,714 --> 00:04:39,190
Enough, on paper, to pay for everything, many times over.

61
00:04:40,333 --> 00:04:45,702
To prove it was real, in December twenty thirteen, Mars One asked the public directly.

62
00:04:45,702 --> 00:04:49,639
A crowdfunding campaign for a robotic scout mission by twenty eighteen.

63
00:04:49,639 --> 00:04:53,577
It raised about four hundred thousand dollars. That scout never flew.

64
00:04:54,699 --> 00:05:00,319
It hired real names, too. Lockheed Martin, paid to study a Mars lander.

65
00:05:00,319 --> 00:05:04,642
Paragon, to study life support. They were only paper studies.

66
00:05:04,642 --> 00:05:08,100
But they bought Mars One something priceless. Credibility.

67
00:05:09,233 --> 00:05:14,618
And to get the cast, in April twenty thirteen, Mars One did something extraordinary.

68
00:05:14,618 --> 00:05:17,694
It opened applications to the entire human race.

69
00:05:17,694 --> 00:05:20,387
Anyone could apply to leave Earth forever.

70
00:05:21,533 --> 00:05:22,972
The response was staggering.

71
00:05:22,972 --> 00:05:31,250
In the first two weeks alone, Mars One said seventy eight thousand people had applied, from more than a hundred and twenty countries.

72
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:38,763
By the time it was over, the number Mars One gave the press was the one that went around the world.

73
00:05:38,763 --> 00:05:43,006
More than two hundred thousand people, it said, wanted a one-way ticket to Mars.

74
00:05:44,133 --> 00:05:45,704
And the media loved it.

75
00:05:45,704 --> 00:05:51,989
A one-way ticket to Mars was an irresistible story, and almost no one paused to check the arithmetic behind it.

76
00:05:51,989 --> 00:05:54,817
The number became a fact simply by being repeated.

77
00:05:55,966 --> 00:05:59,994
And these were not all thrill seekers. They were doctors, teachers, engineers.

78
00:05:59,994 --> 00:06:07,042
People who told their husbands and wives and children that they were training to leave the planet, and never come back.

79
00:06:08,166 --> 00:06:13,093
Take Sonia Van Meter, a political consultant in Texas, married, with two young stepsons.

80
00:06:13,093 --> 00:06:16,612
She applied for a one-way seat, and she meant it.

81
00:06:16,612 --> 00:06:20,835
Her family began, slowly, to prepare for a goodbye with no return.

82
00:06:21,966 --> 00:06:27,761
Others wrote open essays with titles like, why I am volunteering to die on Mars.

83
00:06:27,761 --> 00:06:29,692
These were not gullible fools.

84
00:06:29,692 --> 00:06:34,714
They were sincere, credentialed people, rebuilding their whole identities around a single promise.

85
00:06:35,833 --> 00:06:40,526
In February twenty fifteen, Mars One narrowed the field to its final hundred.

86
00:06:40,526 --> 00:06:45,942
Fifty men, fifty women, from around the world. They called them the Mars One Hundred.

87
00:06:47,066 --> 00:06:53,167
Among the hundred were a British astrophysicist, an Australian sustainability researcher, a physics PhD student.

88
00:06:53,167 --> 00:06:58,455
Ordinary, brilliant people who had quietly decided that Mars was worth dying for.

89
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:03,543
One of them was the astrophysicist in Dublin. His name is Joseph Roche.

90
00:07:03,543 --> 00:07:09,004
And unlike almost everyone else, he had the training to actually check the claims he was being sold.

91
00:07:10,133 --> 00:07:16,611
What he found would take the whole dream apart. It started with a single, simple question.

92
00:07:16,611 --> 00:07:19,041
How many people had really applied?

93
00:07:20,166 --> 00:07:22,204
Chapter three. The number.

94
00:07:23,333 --> 00:07:29,184
Two hundred thousand applicants. It was in every headline. Time magazine, CNN, the BBC.

95
00:07:29,184 --> 00:07:33,364
Nobody stopped to ask what the word applicant actually meant.

96
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:36,735
Because the two hundred thousand were not applicants.

97
00:07:36,735 --> 00:07:40,926
They were people who had left an email address on a website. That was it.

98
00:07:42,066 --> 00:07:46,007
Mars One's own chief medical officer explained the gap this way.

99
00:07:46,007 --> 00:07:50,664
There were hurdles, he said. And the very first hurdle was a payment.

100
00:07:50,664 --> 00:07:52,097
A small administration fee.

101
00:07:53,233 --> 00:07:55,712
A real application meant something completely different.

102
00:07:55,712 --> 00:08:01,732
You had to pay a fee, film a video, submit a medical statement, and answer a questionnaire.

103
00:08:01,732 --> 00:08:02,794
Almost nobody did.

104
00:08:03,933 --> 00:08:09,870
By the close of applications, the number who had actually paid and submitted a video was not two hundred thousand.

105
00:08:09,870 --> 00:08:13,729
According to NBC News, it was two thousand seven hundred and eighty two.

106
00:08:14,866 --> 00:08:16,673
That is not a rounding error.

107
00:08:16,673 --> 00:08:22,095
That is the difference between a global phenomenon and a crowd that would not fill a small theatre.

108
00:08:22,095 --> 00:08:24,505
The headline was off by ninety nine percent.

109
00:08:25,633 --> 00:08:29,821
And inside Mars One, even the numbers disagreed with each other.

110
00:08:29,821 --> 00:08:35,532
Its own chief medical officer told the Guardian he had seen around eighty thousand applications.

111
00:08:35,532 --> 00:08:40,862
Two hundred thousand. Eighty thousand. A few thousand. Not one figure matched the next.

112
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,637
When the criticism grew, Mars One quietly updated its own website.

113
00:08:45,637 --> 00:08:51,258
The true number of completed applications, it now admitted, was four thousand two hundred and twenty seven.

114
00:08:51,258 --> 00:08:55,557
Still barely two percent of the number it had sold to the world.

115
00:08:56,700 --> 00:08:59,508
And even four thousand was only Mars One's own count.

116
00:08:59,508 --> 00:09:05,686
As the journalist Elmo Keep put it, we do not know that four thousand is any more real than two hundred thousand.

117
00:09:05,686 --> 00:09:07,933
It is simply what they are telling us.

118
00:09:09,066 --> 00:09:13,034
And why did so few people finish? Because of the very first hurdle.

119
00:09:13,034 --> 00:09:14,865
To apply, you had to pay.

120
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,722
The application fee was not flat. It was tiered by the wealth of your country.

121
00:09:20,722 --> 00:09:23,556
As little as five dollars in the poorest nations.

122
00:09:23,556 --> 00:09:27,964
Thirty eight dollars in the United States. Up to seventy three in the richest.

123
00:09:29,100 --> 00:09:32,979
Mars One framed this as fairness, making the dream affordable everywhere.

124
00:09:32,979 --> 00:09:38,269
Its critics saw something else. A dream that turned every hopeful into a paying customer.

125
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:42,130
How much did the fees raise? We do not know.

126
00:09:42,130 --> 00:09:45,134
Lansdorp never disclosed it, and no audited accounts were ever published.

127
00:09:45,134 --> 00:09:47,864
But the strangest part was not the money coming in.

128
00:09:47,864 --> 00:09:50,868
It was how you climbed the list once you were in.

129
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:53,802
Chapter four. Points.

130
00:09:54,933 --> 00:09:59,191
This is where Joseph Roche stopped being a finalist, and started being a whistleblower.

131
00:09:59,191 --> 00:10:03,449
Because the deeper he got, the less the selection looked like a space program.

132
00:10:04,566 --> 00:10:10,841
In March twenty fifteen, Roche went on the record with the journalist Elmo Keep, in an investigation for Medium.

133
00:10:10,841 --> 00:10:13,813
What he described was not a rigorous astronaut search.

134
00:10:14,933 --> 00:10:21,522
By Roche's account, his entire vetting to become one of the first humans on Mars came down to this.

135
00:10:21,522 --> 00:10:26,030
A questionnaire. A video. A medical exam he arranged with his own doctor.

136
00:10:26,030 --> 00:10:28,804
And a single ten minute call over Skype.

137
00:10:29,933 --> 00:10:35,324
The selection was supposed to have far more. Group challenges. Isolation tests.

138
00:10:35,324 --> 00:10:37,571
A real, televised astronaut trial.

139
00:10:37,571 --> 00:10:43,412
But round after round was quietly postponed, or cancelled, or simply never arranged.

140
00:10:44,533 --> 00:10:46,231
There was no in-person interview.

141
00:10:46,231 --> 00:10:49,966
The multi-day regional round he had been promised was quietly cancelled.

142
00:10:49,966 --> 00:10:56,079
In his words, it was, quote, just not enough information to make a judgment on someone about anything.

143
00:10:57,200 --> 00:10:59,649
Then came the part that alarmed him most.

144
00:10:59,649 --> 00:11:02,710
Every candidate sat on a public leaderboard, ranked by points.

145
00:11:02,710 --> 00:11:06,996
And Roche said there was really only one way to earn more of them.

146
00:11:08,133 --> 00:11:11,161
You could buy Mars One merchandise. You could donate money.

147
00:11:11,161 --> 00:11:14,491
According to Roche, the more you gave, the higher you climbed.

148
00:11:14,491 --> 00:11:21,455
The list of the most likely people to go to Mars looked, to him, a lot like a list of the biggest donors.

149
00:11:22,600 --> 00:11:32,259
When the Guardian published a list of the top ten most likely Mars One hopefuls, Roche said the ranking was, in large part, a ranking of who had donated the most.

150
00:11:32,259 --> 00:11:35,687
The public faces of the mission were, quietly, its biggest givers.

151
00:11:36,833 --> 00:11:41,075
Watching all of this, some writers reached for a very different comparison.

152
00:11:41,075 --> 00:11:43,196
Not a space agency at all.

153
00:11:43,196 --> 00:11:47,439
Something closer to a multi-level marketing scheme, wearing the costume of exploration.

154
00:11:48,566 --> 00:11:57,395
And Roche said that when reporters paid finalists for interviews, Mars One asked the candidates to donate seventy five percent of that fee straight back to the organization.

155
00:11:58,533 --> 00:12:04,750
And everyone who applied had been automatically enrolled in what Mars One called its community, and handed points.

156
00:12:04,750 --> 00:12:07,858
The entire hopeful crowd had quietly become an audience.

157
00:12:07,858 --> 00:12:10,967
One that could be sold to, and sold things.

158
00:12:12,100 --> 00:12:15,488
This is the moment to be careful, and fair.

159
00:12:15,488 --> 00:12:18,500
These are Roche's allegations, reported by one journalist.

160
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:23,019
They are not a court's findings. And Bas Lansdorp rejected them, flatly.

161
00:12:24,166 --> 00:12:28,527
Donations, Lansdorp said, had nothing to do with who was selected.

162
00:12:28,527 --> 00:12:33,681
Many candidates who gave money did not advance. Many who gave nothing did.

163
00:12:33,681 --> 00:12:37,645
To suggest otherwise, he said, was, quote, simply a lie.

164
00:12:38,766 --> 00:12:44,164
Mars One also insisted its screening was serious, with checks it said were similar to NASA's own.

165
00:12:44,164 --> 00:12:53,055
But when Elmo Keep pressed for specifics, who sat on the committee, where the tests happened, how long they took, she reported that the answers simply never came.

166
00:12:54,200 --> 00:12:58,876
So we are left with a dispute, not a verdict. A respected scientist on one side.

167
00:12:58,876 --> 00:13:00,630
A sincere founder on the other.

168
00:13:00,630 --> 00:13:05,307
But Roche was about to be joined by five engineers who did not deal in opinions.

169
00:13:05,307 --> 00:13:06,477
They dealt in numbers.

170
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:10,108
Chapter five. Sixty eight days.

171
00:13:11,233 --> 00:13:16,068
In twenty fourteen, a team of engineers at MIT did something no journalist could.

172
00:13:16,068 --> 00:13:22,285
They took Mars One's own plan, fed it into a life support simulation, and asked a single question.

173
00:13:22,285 --> 00:13:23,667
Would the colonists survive?

174
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:26,140
They did not guess.

175
00:13:26,140 --> 00:13:31,502
They built their simulation on the only closed habitat humans have ever actually run for real.

176
00:13:31,502 --> 00:13:32,842
The International Space Station.

177
00:13:32,842 --> 00:13:36,529
And even with that as the starting point, the colony died.

178
00:13:37,666 --> 00:13:39,252
This is that study.

179
00:13:39,252 --> 00:13:45,991
An independent assessment, by five MIT engineers, of whether human beings could actually survive Mars One's colony.

180
00:13:45,991 --> 00:13:51,145
Its verdict was brutal. As designed, the mission would kill its own crew.

181
00:13:52,266 --> 00:13:56,920
But here is the cruel twist. The first settler would not die from running out of oxygen.

182
00:13:56,920 --> 00:13:59,110
They would die from too much of it.

183
00:14:00,233 --> 00:14:04,496
To feed the crew, Mars One planned to grow crops inside the habitat.

184
00:14:04,496 --> 00:14:06,464
But those plants breathe out oxygen.

185
00:14:06,464 --> 00:14:12,040
In a sealed base, oxygen would build and build, until the air itself became a fire hazard.

186
00:14:13,166 --> 00:14:17,233
It began with food. Mars One planned about fifty square metres of crops.

187
00:14:17,233 --> 00:14:21,924
MIT calculated you would need closer to two hundred just to keep the crew alive.

188
00:14:21,924 --> 00:14:25,365
And more plants meant more oxygen, and a far bigger problem.

189
00:14:26,500 --> 00:14:30,009
There was no technology in the plan to pull that extra oxygen out.

190
00:14:30,009 --> 00:14:33,788
So the only fix was to vent the air, and replace it with nitrogen.

191
00:14:33,788 --> 00:14:35,408
Except the nitrogen would run out.

192
00:14:36,533 --> 00:14:42,074
And once it did, every attempt to control the oxygen would bleed away the atmosphere itself.

193
00:14:42,074 --> 00:14:46,576
The pressure would fall. And around day sixty eight, the crew would suffocate.

194
00:14:46,576 --> 00:14:48,654
Not in a disaster. On schedule.

195
00:14:49,800 --> 00:14:53,392
Because Mars One's central promise had always been this.

196
00:14:53,392 --> 00:14:57,784
No new major developments or inventions are needed, its plan said.

197
00:14:57,784 --> 00:14:59,780
Only existing, validated, available technology.

198
00:14:59,780 --> 00:15:08,164
MIT checked that claim, system by system, and found several of those technologies did not yet exist at the level required.

199
00:15:09,300 --> 00:15:13,197
And suffocation was only the beginning. The logistics were just as impossible.

200
00:15:13,197 --> 00:15:21,640
To land the gear for the first crews, MIT calculated you would need ten to fourteen of the largest rockets on Earth, for the setup alone.

201
00:15:21,640 --> 00:15:23,589
Mars One had planned for six.

202
00:15:24,733 --> 00:15:29,246
And because nothing ever comes back, the spare parts pile up, mission after mission.

203
00:15:29,246 --> 00:15:37,305
MIT projected that after a couple of decades on the surface, spare parts alone would make up more than half of everything shipped from Earth.

204
00:15:37,305 --> 00:15:39,884
The colony would spend itself just staying alive.

205
00:15:41,033 --> 00:15:42,548
And the bill only grew.

206
00:15:42,548 --> 00:15:51,336
By the time you were resupplying just the third crew, MIT estimated you would need up to twenty four heavy launches, at a cost of over seven billion dollars.

207
00:15:51,336 --> 00:15:54,669
For a single resupply run. And then it would climb again.

208
00:15:55,800 --> 00:15:57,394
Lansdorp fought back hard.

209
00:15:57,394 --> 00:16:03,774
The study, he said, made wrong assumptions and had, quote, nothing to do with our mission.

210
00:16:03,774 --> 00:16:06,964
Oxygen removal, he insisted, already existed in hospitals.

211
00:16:06,964 --> 00:16:09,357
It just needed testing in space.

212
00:16:10,500 --> 00:16:12,661
He offered his own competing numbers.

213
00:16:12,661 --> 00:16:18,785
He could do it, he said, for one point six billion dollars, using thirteen launches, not fifteen.

214
00:16:18,785 --> 00:16:21,667
But on one point, even he gave ground.

215
00:16:21,667 --> 00:16:25,991
His six billion dollar price tag, he admitted, was probably too low.

216
00:16:27,133 --> 00:16:33,972
But then came a public debate, in August twenty fifteen, where the two sides finally met.

217
00:16:33,972 --> 00:16:36,537
And something remarkable happened. They agreed.

218
00:16:37,666 --> 00:16:48,146
And at that debate, Mars One's own technical partner, from the very company it had hired to study its life support, sat across from the MIT team and conceded.

219
00:16:48,146 --> 00:16:50,675
Their analysis, he agreed, was essentially correct.

220
00:16:51,800 --> 00:16:56,406
Mars One's own technical partner conceded the MIT analysis was largely correct.

221
00:16:56,406 --> 00:17:04,469
And Lansdorp himself admitted that the specific plan to land humans by twenty twenty seven was, in his words, mostly fiction.

222
00:17:05,600 --> 00:17:08,064
And then he did something stranger still.

223
00:17:08,064 --> 00:17:12,993
He reframed the study that predicted his colonists suffocating as support for his plan.

224
00:17:12,993 --> 00:17:16,866
I actually consider it, he said, a confirmation of Mars One.

225
00:17:16,866 --> 00:17:21,796
The goal, he insisted, had always been sincere, even when the plan was fiction.

226
00:17:22,933 --> 00:17:26,746
The founder was still sincere. He still wanted humans on Mars.

227
00:17:26,746 --> 00:17:32,986
But he had just admitted, out loud, that the timeline millions of people believed in was not real.

228
00:17:32,986 --> 00:17:35,759
The science was finished. The money was next.

229
00:17:36,900 --> 00:17:39,408
Chapter six. The show that never aired.

230
00:17:40,533 --> 00:17:44,729
Remember, the entire plan to pay for Mars rested on one thing.

231
00:17:44,729 --> 00:17:49,624
The greatest reality show ever made. And in June twenty fourteen, it looked real.

232
00:17:50,766 --> 00:17:58,190
Mars One signed a production company owned by Endemol, the studio behind Big Brother, to film the astronaut selection and training.

233
00:17:58,190 --> 00:18:01,372
The most-watched event in history finally had a broadcaster.

234
00:18:02,500 --> 00:18:05,196
And then, in February twenty fifteen, it collapsed.

235
00:18:05,196 --> 00:18:10,589
The two sides could never agree on a contract. Not a single frame was ever filmed.

236
00:18:10,589 --> 00:18:12,949
The funding model walked out the door.

237
00:18:14,066 --> 00:18:15,919
Mars One tried to salvage it.

238
00:18:15,919 --> 00:18:19,933
Instead of a live global show, it would make its own short films.

239
00:18:19,933 --> 00:18:23,020
But a handful of web videos is not the Olympics.

240
00:18:23,020 --> 00:18:26,108
The money engine was gone, and it never came back.

241
00:18:27,233 --> 00:18:32,366
With the reality show gone, Mars One needed a new way to look like a real business.

242
00:18:32,366 --> 00:18:35,385
So in twenty sixteen, it did something clever, and hollow.

243
00:18:35,385 --> 00:18:37,499
It went public on a stock exchange.

244
00:18:38,633 --> 00:18:40,310
Not through a normal listing.

245
00:18:40,310 --> 00:18:47,355
Through a reverse takeover of a dormant Swiss shell company called InFin, a former mobile payments firm that had gone quiet.

246
00:18:47,355 --> 00:18:51,381
On paper, the deal valued Mars One at eighty seven million euros.

247
00:18:52,500 --> 00:18:54,585
InFin was a dormant former mobile-payments firm.

248
00:18:54,585 --> 00:18:59,352
After the merger, the old Mars One owners kept ninety seven percent of the new stock.

249
00:18:59,352 --> 00:19:01,139
They had not really raised money.

250
00:19:01,139 --> 00:19:05,013
They had merged a dream into an empty shell, and called it public.

251
00:19:06,133 --> 00:19:08,016
But paper is not cash.

252
00:19:08,016 --> 00:19:13,288
When the shares actually started trading, in Frankfurt, they opened at sixteen euro cents.

253
00:19:13,288 --> 00:19:19,690
The real market value of the company that promised to colonize Mars was under three million euros.

254
00:19:20,833 --> 00:19:23,484
Follow the actual money and it was tiny.

255
00:19:23,484 --> 00:19:28,122
Donations and merchandise, by Mars One's own count, came to under a million dollars.

256
00:19:28,122 --> 00:19:31,104
A crowdfunding campaign raised about three hundred thousand more.

257
00:19:31,104 --> 00:19:33,424
Against a mission that needed six billion.

258
00:19:34,566 --> 00:19:39,383
And the structure underneath was stranger than it looked. There were two Mars Ones.

259
00:19:39,383 --> 00:19:44,200
A Dutch non-profit that owned the mission. And a for-profit that owned the money.

260
00:19:44,200 --> 00:19:48,672
The for-profit promised the mission just five cents of every dollar it earned.

261
00:19:49,800 --> 00:19:51,746
Follow that math to the end.

262
00:19:51,746 --> 00:19:59,854
To fund a six billion euro mission on a five percent cut, the company would have had to earn a hundred and twenty billion euros.

263
00:19:59,854 --> 00:20:02,449
More than almost any media franchise in history.

264
00:20:02,449 --> 00:20:05,369
The business model was, quietly, impossible from the start.

265
00:20:06,500 --> 00:20:13,504
By independent accounts, the company was running on almost nothing, and had been in debt for years, according to Dutch filings.

266
00:20:13,504 --> 00:20:19,509
Its own chief medical officer said, plainly, that he had no clue what the finances at all were.

267
00:20:20,633 --> 00:20:23,120
And the money that did come in?

268
00:20:23,120 --> 00:20:28,448
By Mars One's own accounting, most of it went to concept studies, travel, and marketing.

269
00:20:28,448 --> 00:20:33,067
Its critics had a blunter version. It went into videos, not a spaceship.

270
00:20:34,200 --> 00:20:35,849
And the deadlines kept sliding.

271
00:20:35,849 --> 00:20:43,106
The first landing moved from twenty twenty three, to twenty twenty five, to twenty twenty seven, and finally to twenty thirty one.

272
00:20:43,106 --> 00:20:45,746
Always just far enough away to keep believing.

273
00:20:46,866 --> 00:20:51,195
No rocket was ever built. No robotic probe ever flew.

274
00:20:51,195 --> 00:20:56,390
The colony existed only in press releases and beautifully rendered concept art.

275
00:20:56,390 --> 00:20:58,987
And then, very quietly, it ended.

276
00:21:00,133 --> 00:21:02,641
Chapter seven. A quiet bankruptcy.

277
00:21:03,766 --> 00:21:07,412
There was no press conference. No apology to the two hundred thousand.

278
00:21:07,412 --> 00:21:11,968
No message to the hundred finalists who had told their families they were leaving Earth.

279
00:21:13,100 --> 00:21:22,362
On the fifteenth of January, twenty nineteen, at three thirty seven in the afternoon, a civil court in Basel, Switzerland, declared the company bankrupt.

280
00:21:22,362 --> 00:21:25,064
The dream had a time of death.

281
00:21:26,200 --> 00:21:31,947
The for-profit arm that was supposed to raise billions had debts of roughly one million euros.

282
00:21:31,947 --> 00:21:36,616
And in its accounts, according to reporting, less than twenty five thousand dollars.

283
00:21:36,616 --> 00:21:39,131
Less than the price of a car.

284
00:21:40,266 --> 00:21:42,392
Sit with the size of that gap.

285
00:21:42,392 --> 00:21:52,112
A company that once said it would spend six billion dollars, and earn tens of billions more, had ended with about a million euros in debt and almost nothing in the bank.

286
00:21:52,112 --> 00:21:55,757
The distance between the promise and the reality was the entire story.

287
00:21:56,900 --> 00:22:00,022
And the strangest detail of all. Almost nobody noticed.

288
00:22:00,022 --> 00:22:05,573
For nearly a month, the death of Mars One sat unread in a Swiss court register.

289
00:22:06,700 --> 00:22:13,030
The world only found out in February, when an anonymous user on Reddit stumbled across the filing and posted a link.

290
00:22:13,030 --> 00:22:17,854
That is how the mission to colonize Mars ended. As a link on a message board.

291
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,202
For the hundred finalists, there was no letter.

292
00:22:21,202 --> 00:22:28,086
Many of them learned that the mission they had rebuilt their whole lives around was dead the same way the rest of the world did.

293
00:22:28,086 --> 00:22:29,188
From a news story.

294
00:22:30,333 --> 00:22:37,126
Bas Lansdorp says the non-profit foundation still legally exists, that it is only stalled, that he is still looking for an investor.

295
00:22:37,126 --> 00:22:42,375
As of today, no one has ever gone to Mars with Mars One. No one ever will.

296
00:22:43,500 --> 00:22:49,282
And let us be precise about what this was, and what it was not. Mars One went bankrupt.

297
00:22:49,282 --> 00:22:52,816
That is insolvency. It is not a court finding of fraud.

298
00:22:52,816 --> 00:22:55,386
Bas Lansdorp was never charged with a crime.

299
00:22:56,533 --> 00:23:01,500
Critics called it a scam. A money grab. The fake mission that fooled the world.

300
00:23:01,500 --> 00:23:06,799
But those are their words, not a verdict. The truth sits in a harder, greyer place.

301
00:23:07,933 --> 00:23:12,833
So what do we do with a story like this? Because here is what stays with me.

302
00:23:12,833 --> 00:23:16,292
It is not the missing money. It is not even the physics.

303
00:23:17,433 --> 00:23:20,094
It is how badly people wanted to go.

304
00:23:20,094 --> 00:23:28,744
Two hundred thousand of them raised their hands to leave everything they had ever known, forever, to stand on red dirt under a small, cold sun.

305
00:23:29,866 --> 00:23:33,022
Some of them had already begun to let go.

306
00:23:33,022 --> 00:23:37,582
Sonia Van Meter's family had started, gently, to picture a life without her.

307
00:23:37,582 --> 00:23:41,790
That is what a ticket like this really costs. Not just money.

308
00:23:41,790 --> 00:23:43,894
Years of a real person's hope.

309
00:23:45,033 --> 00:23:46,806
Joseph Roche never wanted revenge.

310
00:23:46,806 --> 00:23:56,738
He said his nightmare was simply that people would keep giving their money and their hope to something that would, in his words, inevitably fall on its face.

311
00:23:56,738 --> 00:23:58,512
In twenty nineteen, it did.

312
00:23:59,633 --> 00:24:02,021
Most of the hundred quietly moved on.

313
00:24:02,021 --> 00:24:05,775
Some kept working toward Mars, on real missions, with real agencies.

314
00:24:05,775 --> 00:24:10,552
The longing did not die with the company. It just needed an honest vehicle.

315
00:24:11,700 --> 00:24:16,700
The dream of Mars was real. The longing was real. It was only the ticket that was fake.

316
00:24:16,700 --> 00:24:23,089
And maybe the most human thing in this whole story is how many of us wanted to believe the ticket was real anyway.

317
00:24:24,233 --> 00:24:32,993
If a mission that never left the ground can pull in two hundred thousand people, wait until you see what happened to the astronauts who actually flew, and never came home.

318
00:24:32,993 --> 00:24:36,667
Watch our film on the twenty one astronauts who died reaching for space.

319
00:24:37,800 --> 00:24:40,288
If this story moved you, subscribe to mindwired.

320
00:24:40,288 --> 00:24:46,510
We tell the true stories of how far people will go to touch the stars, and what it costs them.

321
00:24:46,510 --> 00:24:48,066
This has been Mars One.

